---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prompt from '../components/Prompt.astro';
import { INSTALL_PROMPT, REPO } from '../data/site';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<Base title="Desks: a way of working for Claude Code" description="Work sorted by topic, answers checked by other AI vendors before you read them, and a written close. Open source, for Claude Code.">
  <span class="status">Free and open source. Early version, tested daily by one person, now opening to others.</span>
  <h1>Claude's answers, checked by other AIs before you read them.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Desks is a way of working for Claude Code. Your work sorted by topic, recommendations attacked by models from other vendors, heavy reading sent to cheaper workers, and a written close so the next session starts where this one stopped.</p>

  <Prompt id="install" text={INSTALL_PROMPT} caption="Open your folder in Claude Code and paste this" />
  <p class="note">It copies one skill and three workers into the folder and adds one marked block to <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. It asks before touching anything you already have. <a href={`${base}install/`}>What the prompt does, step by step.</a></p>

  <h2>What a session looks like</h2>
  <div class="chat">
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">You</div><p><code>desk sell-camera</code></p></div>
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">Claude</div><p>sell-camera · new · history on · review: cross-vendor. What are we selling, and by when?</p></div>
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">You</div><p>My old DSLR kit, this month. What should I ask for it?</p></div>
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">Claude</div>
      <p>Ask 95, accept 80. My first number was 120, taken from what similar kits are listed at. Two of three reviewers pointed out that unsold listings show what does <em>not</em> sell. Sold ones went for 70 to 100.</p>
      <p>One reviewer suggested offering shipping. I did not follow that: you asked for a quick sale, and disputes on a shipped old camera cost more than the extra reach.</p>
      <p class="receipt">Review: [converged] · 3 vendors · receipt 5d1c0a77e2</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="note">An invented example. <a href={`${base}examples/`}>See the files this session leaves behind.</a></p>

  <h2>Write the way you think</h2>
  <p>You do not have to prepare a clean brief. A Desk is built for input like this:</p>
  <div class="chat">
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">You</div>
      <p>ok so the garage thing.. my sister says saturday but i think the 14th is the school fair?? not sure<br />
      also the bikes, are they even worth selling or just give them away. and the neighbour said something about a permit<br />
      oh and dont post anything yet</p>
    </div>
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">Claude</div>
      <p><strong>garage-sale</strong> · new. I heard four things:</p>
      <p>1. <strong>The date.</strong> I checked: the school fair is on the 14th, 9 to 1. The Saturday after is free. Proposal: the 21st.<br />
      2. <strong>The bikes.</strong> Two similar ones sold nearby for 40 to 60 last month. Worth selling, as a pair.<br />
      3. <strong>The permit.</strong> I could not confirm this either way from the town's site. That one needs a phone call, and it is yours to make.<br />
      4. <strong>Nothing gets posted.</strong> Noted. Drafts only until you say so.</p>
      <p class="receipt">Review: [converged] · 3 vendors · receipt 8a41f0c2d7</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="note">An invented example. Claude sorts what you said, checks what can be checked, says what it could not verify, and keeps the "don't" as a rule for the whole topic. If you had also mentioned your tax return, it would have filed that under its own topic and carried on.</p>

  <h2>Short requests, completed in the open</h2>
  <p>Every assistant adds things to what you type. A Desk shows you what it added, and where each addition came from, before it starts:</p>
  <div class="chat">
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">You</div><p>Draft the letter about the leak.</p></div>
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">Claude</div>
      <p><strong>Doing:</strong> draft the letter about the leak<br />
      <span class="added"><strong>+ topic notes</strong> (README, What we learned): to the landlord · firm, not hostile</span><br />
      <span class="added"><strong>+ your profile</strong> (Never without asking): no legal threats</span><br />
      <span class="added"><strong>+ my assumption:</strong> a second letter, since the notes say the first got no answer</span><br />
      <span class="added"><strong>+ Desk:</strong> a draft only; nothing is sent</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="turn"><div class="who">You</div><p>no, this one goes to the building manager</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="note">An invented example. A guess is labelled as a guess, never as a rule. As the notes on a topic grow, what you have to type shrinks. It shows what Desks and your own files added: the instructions Claude gets from its maker are not ours to show.</p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>Reviewed answers</h3><p>Advice and anything high-stakes goes, with names removed, to free models from other vendors. Claude has to answer each objection, and the reply says how it was reviewed.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>It leads</h3><p>It opens with where things stand and the move it would make next, carries the good habits for you, and offers what you did not ask for when it has evidence. One sentence turns that off.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Proposals, not questions</h3><p>Claude settles what it can and brings a reviewed proposal. It waits for your word on five things: money, anything public, contacting someone, access, and anything permanent.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>One topic, one folder</h3><p>State, decisions and a note per session, in plain files. History, branches and pull requests happen under the hood. You never type git.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Your plan lasts longer</h3><p>Reading, screen work and long outputs go to workers on a smaller model. The session proposes its own close before it gets expensive.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>It picks up where it stopped</h3><p>At the close, the topic's notes are rewritten so a fresh session can continue from them alone.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>It fits around your setup</h3><p>Your rules win. Desk adds to what you have and can use your folders, your agents, and other AI subscriptions you already pay for as reviewers.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Free, and yours to change</h2>
  <p>Desks is open source under the MIT licence. It costs nothing, there is no account and nothing to subscribe to, and the review runs on models that are free. You pay only for the assistant you already use. Read every line, change any of it, and keep your changes.</p>

  <h2>What it needs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tested with:</strong> Claude Code on a paid Claude plan (the desktop app's Code tab, or the terminal), on a Mac. That is the only setup it has been run on.</li>
    <li><strong>Not tied to it:</strong> the contract is plain instructions and the scripts are plain Python. Any capable assistant that can read files and run commands, paid or free, can be put to the test. Nobody has yet. If you do, tell us how it went.</li>
    <li>For cross-vendor review: Python 3 and a free OpenRouter key. Without them, a second Claude reviews instead, and the reply says so.</li>
    <li>Nothing else. Git and GitHub are used when they are there.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href={`${base}how/`}>How it works</a> · <a href={REPO}>Source on GitHub</a></p>
</Base>
